{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>If like the death of all men.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory compared the matter to what is it like? To the best man (שושבין) of the king's daughter, who held the evidence in his hand. One of the guests arose and cursed the best man, saying, 'The king's daughter was not a virgin.' The best man stood before the king and said to him, 'If you do not avenge me against this man, then I too say: surely no virginity was found for the king's daughter.' The king said, 'It is better to kill this man than have the trusted one cast a bad name upon my daughter.' So said Moses: If they die upon their beds in the manner that all men die, and the doctors visit them like all the sick, then I too retract and say: HaShem did not send me, but I did everything on my own initiative, and Korah is justly disputing me. And so said Micah (1 Kings 22:28): 'If you return in peace, then I too say that HaShem did not speak through me.' And so said Elijah (1 Kings 18): 'Today it shall be known that You are Elohim in Israel and I am Your servant, and by Your word I have done all these things... Answer me, O HaShem, answer me, and let this people know that You are HaShem the Elohim, and that You have turned their hearts backward' — that is, if You do not answer me, I say that You turned their hearts backward. And it is a well-known thing that this dispute was the cause of nullifying the entire Torah, for this one disputes him today in one matter and that one disputes him tomorrow in another, and so from matter to matter until he comes to deny the root and say there is no Torah from Heaven, but rather Moses fabricated it all from his own heart. Therefore their punishment was severe. And since they came against the Torah, which is the enduring foundation of heaven and earth, as it is written (Jeremiah 33:25), 'If not for My covenant day and night,' they were punished with a severe punishment from heaven and from earth: from the earth by swallowing, and from heaven by fire. Now this is true and correct according to the plain sense, for they deserved a great and severe punishment in this, and they brought it upon themselves, for whoever rebels against the words of Moses is as one who rebels against the Shekhinah." "<b>But.</b> One must ask and wonder a great wonder regarding this matter on two points. The first: Moses, who was a faithful shepherd and prayed for Israel many times for many transgressions they committed — as in the matter of the Golden Calf and the sin of the spies and others — how did he not pray for them here, and how did he not restore them to the right path? And surely repentance avails over all, and there is no sin in the entire Torah, however severe, that has no remedy through repentance, for His right hand is extended to receive those who return, even to the rebels and the wicked against Him. And if they had doubts about Moses's words, and their thought concerning him was that he was distributing appointments to positions and authorities according to his own will and not from HaShem, may He be exalted, it would have been possible for Moses to give them a sign so that they would believe his words, for they were astute and great sages, and through that sign it would become clear to them that Aaron is the holy one chosen for the rank of priesthood, and likewise they would believe in the rest of the appointments to positions — that all was said to him from the mouth of the Almighty and Moses did not fabricate it from his heart. And furthermore, if they sinned and rebelled against HaShem and against Moses His servant, and they brought all this punishment upon themselves, what sin did the little ones lying in the cradle commit, that they were included in this punishment, as it is written: 'and their wives and their sons and their little ones' (ונשיהם ובניהם וטפם) — and the little ones (והטף), when did they sin? 'And HaShem is righteous; He does no injustice' (והשם צדיק לא יעשה עולה)." "<b>Now then.</b> If we seek to fit this passage according to the plain sense (peshat), all this will be difficult for us, but it is impossible to fit it except by way of the Kabbalah that is faithful. And I will hint hints; hear them and know them for yourself. You will find regarding the generation of the Dispersion (Genesis 11:4): 'Come, let us build for ourselves a city and a tower with its head in the heavens, and let us make for ourselves a name,' and it is written of them: 'and this is what they began to do.' And you will find regarding the men of Sodom (Genesis 19): 'and the men of the city, the men of Sodom, surrounded the house,' and Scripture explained that the men of the city and the tower are the men of Sodom, and it says of them: 'and the men who were at the entrance of the house they struck with blindness' (ואת האנשים אשר פתח הבית הכו בסנורים). And you find 'the men of Sodom and the men of the city,' and in this passage: 'the congregation of Korah — and they rose up before Moses' (ובעדת קרח ויקומו לפני משה). Now it should have said 'against Moses' (על משה), but it hints that they were 'before Moses' (לפני משה), and because of this he called them 'men of name' (אנשי שם), and Onkelos translated ויקח as ואתפליג ('and he was divided'), hinting at the generation of the Dispersion (דור הפלגה). And here it mentions in their punishment: 'the eyes of those men were gouged out' (העיני האנשים ההם תנקר) — as one who frightens his fellow with a blow that has already come upon him. And to what may the matter be compared? To a compassionate father who had a foolish son who does not behave properly. The first time, he rebuked him; the second, he struck and chastised him; the third, he despairs of him and abandons him to his own wickedness. So too the generation of the Dispersion, of whom it is written 'and this is what they began to do' — what did He do? He scattered them (הפיצם). And the men of Sodom — He struck them with blindness (הכם בסנורים). And the congregation of Korah — they perished from the world, for they deserved to be uprooted from the world. And behold, the intention of HaShem, may His name be exalted, who does kindness to thousands, was nullified concerning them. And regarding the secret of this matter, Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 4:9): 'Two are better than one,' and he mentioned after it 'and the threefold cord' (והחוט המשולש), which is the third time. And from the fact that he said 'it is not quickly severed' (לא במהרה ינתק), you learn that sometimes it is severed even on the third, but that is rare and not a common occurrence, and therefore he said 'it is not quickly severed.'" "<b>And in the book.</b> In the Sefer ha-Bahir we find explicitly: Rabbi Akiva said, what is the meaning of what is written (Ecclesiastes 1:4): 'A generation comes and a generation goes' (דור הולך ודור בא) — that has already come. To what may the matter be compared? To a king who had servants and clothed them in garments of silk and embroidery according to his ability. They corrupted their conduct; he cast them away and pushed them from before him and stripped them, and they went on their way. He took the garments and washed them thoroughly until no blemish remained in them, and laid them aside ready at hand. He acquired other servants and clothed them in those garments, and he did not know whether they would be better than those former servants or not, and thus they merited garments that had already come into the world and been worn by others before them. End of quote." "<b>And from now on.</b> The passage will be made clear to you most well, and the difficulty will be removed entirely — both from the fact that Moses did not pray for them, and from the fact that the punishment fell also upon the little ones. And understand this. And perhaps it is to this that our Rabbis of blessed memory hinted when they said in the Midrash on Psalms (Psalms 46:1): 'For the leader, among the sons of Korah, on Alamot, a song' (למנצח לבני קרח על עלמות שיר) — thus said the sons of Korah: 'From of old' (מעולמין) — these are the things we saw, and we do not know what we saw.'" "<b>And in the Midrash.</b> Rabbi Berechiah said: Come and see how severe is discord, for a court below does not punish until one has brought two hairs (i.e., reached physical maturity), which is at thirteen years and one day, and the court above until twenty years — yet here even nursing infants were destroyed, as it is said: ״ותבלע אותם ואת בתיהם ואת כל האדם אשר לקרח״ — and ״וירדו הם וכל אשר להם חיים שאולה.״ And the word אותם refers back to Dathan and Abiram, mentioned above — ״ונשיהם ובניהם וטפם״ — for they were all standing there, young and old, at that assembly."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {2 "In the prooftext 'הכם בסנורים,' the expected biblical form is 'הכם' (with prefixed conjunctive vav: והכם). Retained R. Bahya's form as written. The full citation from Qohelet 4:12 reads 'והחוט המשולש לא במהרה ינתק'; R. Bahya cites only the last two words.", 3 "R. Bahya introduces the quotation with 'ובספר הבהיר' but omits the book's name; it is the Sefer ha-Bahir, as the content makes clear."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:33:58.370378775Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}